{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV and later acted as a key advisor to Baldwin V. His life involved constant struggle against external threats from Muslim forces while managing internal power struggles within the Crusader states. He faced many challenges because of his long minority rule and his complex relationship with the king.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born around 1140 as the son of Raymond II who was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. His father died when Raymond was a child which forced the kingdom to appoint a regent until he came of age. He inherited the title of Count and the strategic city of Tripoli along with its surrounding territories. His youth made him vulnerable to political maneuvering by other powerful nobles in the region. He eventually assumed full control of his lands and began to expand his influence across the Levant.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond became the regent for Baldwin IV after the king became a leper at a young age. This role placed him at the center of political decision making for the entire Kingdom of Jerusalem. He clashed with Guy of Lusignan over the direction of the kingdom and the handling of Saladin. Raymond tried to maintain peace through diplomacy but often found himself at odds with the more aggressive factions at court. He allied with Saladin at one point to protect his own interests against the ambitions of Guy and Raymond of Antioch. This political flexibility allowed him to survive various crises but also earned him enemies who questioned his loyalty.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\nRaymond died in 1187 just before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin occurred. His death removed a crucial stabilizing force from the kingdom at its most vulnerable moment. The subsequent defeat of 